\section{Start from Intensional Stream Equations}

Lucid treats variables as streams and uses operators such as \texttt{fby}, \texttt{first}, and \texttt{next} to express iteration without assignments. Ashcroft and Wadge give equations such as counters and running calculations in this style \cite{wadge1985lucid}. Consider a small Lucid-like program:

\begin{verbatim}
where
  n   = 1 fby n + 1;
  sum = 0 fby sum + input;
  avg = (next sum) / n;
end
\end{verbatim}

At time \(0\), \texttt{n} is \(1\) and \texttt{sum} is \(0\). A demand for \texttt{avg} at context \(t\) asks for \texttt{sum} at \(t+1\), which recursively reaches \texttt{sum} at \(t\) and \texttt{input} at \(t\). The exact alignment of \texttt{next sum} and \(n\) is part of the language semantics. Treating \texttt{fby} as an ordinary eager binary operator would demand its recursive tail while constructing time zero and fail to represent the intended stream.

\section{Build a Demand Graph Before Choosing Streaming Code}

The Lucid-like IR design starts by resolving equations to stable definition IDs and lowering each expression to an intensional graph. A demand key contains node ID and context, here a time index. \texttt{fby} dispatches on that context: its initial branch at zero and its tail branch at successor times. Memoization turns repeated demands for \((\texttt{sum},t)\) into one evaluation. Input is an effectful demand whose sequence contract must be retained.

