The initial output artifacts are a resolved demand graph, context-transfer functions, memo schema, and an interpreter. Analysis then recognizes forward-only affine time demands and finite recurrence state. It can emit a streaming loop, state layout, input/output protocol, and provenance from loop variables back to equations. Unsupported multidimensional or noncausal demands remain on the eductive runtime.

% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Specialize the Demand Graph to a Streaming Loop}

\begin{verbatim}
n := 1
sum := 0
for each input_token:
    next_sum := sum + input_token
    avg := next_sum / n
    emit avg
    sum := next_sum
    n := n + 1
\end{verbatim}

This loop is legal because the demanded contexts advance monotonically, each recurrence needs only the preceding state, and the output at an iteration uses exactly the corresponding \texttt{next sum}. The state slots implement delayed stream values; they are not mutable source variables. Common demand elimination and memoization are legal in the pure graph. In generated streaming code, fusion with an upstream actor, vectorization, or eliminating memo tables additionally requires preserving input order, division behavior, overflow, and output timing. Parallel prefix techniques need an associative state-composition law and a compatible numerical contract.

Validation first compares the demand interpreter and stream loop for every prefix of finite input sequences, including the empty prefix and division or overflow edge cases. It checks that loop state after \(k\) firings equals the graph values needed for context \(k\). A trace records demand keys, memo hits, consumed input index, and emitted output; translation validation maps each loop update to its defining \texttt{fby} equation. Thus the final artifacts---streaming code, state layout, and protocol---are justified by the Lucid-like demand semantics rather than by superficial resemblance to a pipeline.

\section{Replay Prefixes and Test the Specialization Boundary}

For input \(3,5,10\), the graph answers demands for \texttt{avg} at indices \(0,1,2\) with \(3,4,6\). The loop's state at the start of index \(k\) is the pair \((n[k],\texttt{sum}[k])\); the result uses \texttt{sum} at index \(k+1\) and \texttt{n} at index \(k\). This distinction avoids a common off-by-one error where the loop increments the denominator before dividing.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{crrrr}
\hline
Index & Old sum & Input & Divisor & Output \\
\hline
0 & 0 & 3 & 1 & 3 \\
1 & 3 & 5 & 2 & 4 \\
2 & 8 & 10 & 3 & 6 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{verbatim}
check_prefix(input):
    reference := new_demand_graph(input)
    actual := run_loop_from_seed(input)
    require count(actual.outputs) == count(input)
    for k in 0 .. count(input)-1:
        require actual.before[k] == (reference.at(n,k),
                                      reference.at(sum,k))
        require actual.outputs[k] == reference.at(avg,k)
    require actual.final == (reference.at(n,count(input)),
                              reference.at(sum,count(input)))
\end{verbatim}

Run the checker on the empty prefix too; its final state must be the seed pair, and neither evaluator needs an input token. Add a fixture with a nonintegral average to exercise the chosen language's division semantics. A final state record suffices to resume the next sequential index, but cannot answer arbitrary old requests without retaining results or replaying input. If clients can request index 100 and then index 2, document how the API answers the second demand or do not apply this specialization. Sustained streaming throughput does not justify replacing a more general demand interface.

\paragraph{Check resumption against an uninterrupted run.}
Split \(3,5,10\) after the second token. Save the post-step state
\((3,8)\), start the next invocation from that state, and expect
the suffix output to be \(6\). Resetting either field would fail
the check. If the runtime serializes state at a checkpoint, include
the consumed input position in the protocol so replay does not
accidentally feed the last item of the preceding chunk twice.
